WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.730
A student's assistant agent is asked

2
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.510
for a free room on Tuesday.

3
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.170
It confidently names a room

4
00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.250
that does not exist.

5
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.400
It never called the booking tool it had.

6
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:13.540
Why not?

7
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.390
Today: how classical planners search

8
00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.330
for action sequences, how

9
00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:21.570
language-model agents interleave

10
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.550
reasoning with tool calls, and how to

11
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.910
teach students to build and debug both.

12
00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.610
Planning finds a sequence of

13
00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.550
actions that takes the world from

14
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.370
a start state to a goal.

15
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:38.230
Each action has preconditions and effects.

16
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.110
A planner searches this space

17
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.050
instead of guessing.

18
00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.630
A star search expands the node

19
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.130
with the lowest cost so far plus

20
00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.790
estimated cost to go.

21
00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.850
If the estimate never overestimates,

22
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.970
the plan it finds is optimal.

23
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.050
Students can trace it by

24
00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.510
hand on a small map.

25
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.950
A ReAct agent writes a thought,

26
00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.350
calls a tool, and reads the

27
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.510
observation before its next step.

28
00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:08.650
Here it checks the booking

29
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:09.930
system before answering.

30
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.320
The answer is grounded in

31
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.260
what the tool returned.

32
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.130
A language model may answer from memory

33
00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.270
instead of calling a tool, especially if

34
00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.870
the tool's description is vague.

35
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.660
Clear tool descriptions, explicit

36
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.460
instructions, and checking every answer

37
00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.780
against a tool result all reduce this.

38
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.780
Students now meet agents through language

39
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.120
models and skip classical planning.

40
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:37.300
Teach both.

41
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.940
Search gives guarantees and a

42
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.400
vocabulary of states and goals.

43
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.890
Language-model agents give

44
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.350
flexibility, and the classical

45
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.090
ideas explain their failures.

46
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:51.830
Quick check!

47
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.490
A star uses a heuristic that sometimes

48
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.410
overestimates the remaining cost.

49
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.360
Is the plan it finds

50
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.100
guaranteed to be optimal?

51
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:02.660
No.

52
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.450
Optimality needs an admissible

53
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.430
heuristic, one that never overestimates.

54
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.430
With an overestimate, A star

55
00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.090
may skip the best path.

56
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.460
In the app, open the Plan in steps lab.

57
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.860
Pick a goal that needs two tools, guess

58
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.300
the plan, run it, and see what Darsi Tiny

59
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.580
does on Saturday, when the first step

60
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:26.980
fails.

61
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.080
Your turn.

62
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.280
Which change most directly stops the

63
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.280
agent from inventing a room?

64
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.560
Requiring the answer to come from a

65
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.640
tool result, and checking it.

66
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.530
Remember: planners search with

67
00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.190
heuristics, ReAct interleaves

68
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.490
thought with tools, and answers

69
00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.210
should be grounded in observations.

70
00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:53.650
Try it in your next lecture: trace A star

71
00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.390
on a six-node campus map on the board.

72
00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.310
Then show a ReAct transcript

73
00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.070
with one missing tool call.

74
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.640
Pairs find the step where the

75
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.280
agent should have acted.
